{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The target's proposed evidence — low inter-rater variance plus causal-language transcripts — fails to distinguish genuine reliability from mere compliance, because a checklist can be algorithmically rigid enough to force both low variance AND rote-but-uniform causal justifications. The target itself concedes this flaw in its own counterconditions (the 'algorithmically rigid' case), then fails to resolve it: it offers no evidence criterion that separates a panel that converged on a shared causal understanding from a panel that converged because the checklist dictated the same causal narrative. Thus under the target's own standard, a compliant panel mechanically reproducing checklist-linked causal language would be misclassified as a genuine reliability gain within a single evaluation.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Checklist item 4 reads: 'Identify which feature caused the outcome and state why.' Three non-experts independently write: 'Feature X caused the outcome because checklist step 4 says to identify the causal feature.' Inter-rater variance = 0; transcripts contain the words 'caused' and 'because.' Target classifies this as genuine reliability gain.","refutation":"Low variance and causal-language presence are artifacts of checklist rigidity, not evidence of causal understanding, so the target's discriminating evidence does not actually distinguish the two cases it claims to separate."}]}]}